# The libuv release this tree builds `aion/uv` against.
#
# Same doctrine as coalton.pin: the REPO declares the version, so adopting a new
# one is an ordinary commit that propagates through git like everything else. A
# native dependency that each machine resolves independently is the "works on
# mine, not on yours" hazard with an added twist -- a C ABI mismatch does not
# fail politely, it corrupts memory.
#
# We do NOT depend on a distro package. libuv is FETCHED AND BUILT FROM SOURCE by
#   sbcl --script scripts/build-libuv.lisp
# which compiles it with `cc` directly -- no make, no cmake, no autotools (the
# house rule: `sbcl --script` is the only build driver). The one prerequisite is
# a C compiler, which every machine that ships a desktop bundle already has.
#
# Why from source rather than `apt install libuv1-dev`:
#   1. ADR-0011. Woo bound libev at load time, so every Linux/macOS desktop
#      bundle died on a clean machine with "libev.so.4: cannot open shared
#      object file". A native dep we do not build is a native dep we cannot
#      bundle, and it fails at the user, not at us.
#   2. One version everywhere. Distro libuv is 1.51.0 on Ubuntu 26.04 and
#      whatever brew/MSYS2 happens to carry elsewhere; three platforms on three
#      versions is a matrix we would be debugging rather than using.
#   3. It is the same artifact CI publishes and the installers bundle (#78).
#
# 1.52.1 is the current stable release, and is also what MSYS2/ucrt64 ships on
# Windows -- so the Windows machine can cross-check our build against a prebuilt
# of the identical version.

version 1.52.1
sha256  66d511b9e6e334c0e62279eb234fbfb2b3110b1479c09b95b44c7afca8cff9e7
url     https://dist.libuv.org/dist/v1.52.1/libuv-v1.52.1.tar.gz

# On the checksum. Recorded trust-on-first-use: fetched over TLS from the
# official host (dist.libuv.org) and hashed here. Every later fetch on every
# machine is verified against this line, which is what the pin is for -- it
# turns a silent substitution into a loud failure.
#
# Upstream also publishes a detached GPG signature next to the tarball
# (libuv-v1.52.1.tar.gz.sign). Verifying it requires the release key and a trust
# decision about that key, so it is deliberately NOT in the build path; anyone
# who wants that stronger guarantee can take it by hand.
#
# To adopt a new libuv:
#   1. curl -sLO https://dist.libuv.org/dist/vX.Y.Z/libuv-vX.Y.Z.tar.gz
#   2. sha256sum libuv-vX.Y.Z.tar.gz
#   3. update the three lines above, delete vendor/libuv/, rebuild, run the
#      aion/uv suite on all three OSes before committing.

# --- staying current -------------------------------------------------------
#
# Where an advisory would appear, recorded here because the answer is
# per-library and looking it up under time pressure is the wrong moment.
# libuv publishes security fixes as ordinary releases and announces them on its
# GitHub releases page and security advisories tab.
#
# REVIEWED is the date a human last read that page. It is the only thing that
# distinguishes "no advisory affects us" from "nobody has looked" -- two states
# that are otherwise identical from outside, and only one of them is fine.
# Bump it when you look, even when the answer is "nothing to do".

advisories https://github.com/libuv/libuv/security/advisories
reviewed   2026-09-15
